1. Supported Hardware
Board	Chip macro	CAN buses usable	CAN FD?	Hardware mailboxes (CAN 2.0)
Teensy 3.1 / 3.2	__MK20DX256__	CAN0	No	16
Teensy 3.5	__MK64FX512__	CAN0	No	16
Teensy 3.6	__MK66FX1M0__	CAN0, CAN1	No	16
Teensy 4.0 / 4.1	__IMXRT1062__	CAN1, CAN2, CAN3	CAN3 only	64
Notes:

On Teensy 3.x, the CAN1 symbol technically exists in the register map for all chips in that family, but it is only physically wired out on the Teensy 3.6; on 3.2/3.5 it isn't usable even though it compiles.
On Teensy 4, only CAN3 has a CAN FD-capable controller; CAN1/CAN2 are CAN 2.0-only.
Teensy LC has no CAN peripheral at all and isn't supported.
2. Installation
Download or clone the repository into your Arduino libraries folder (or install via the Arduino/PlatformIO Library Manager, where it's listed as FlexCAN_T4).
Include it in your sketch:
#include <FlexCAN_T4.h>
That single include pulls in the message structs, all the enums, and — depending on which chip macro is defined by your board selection — the correct low-level register header (imxrt_flexcan.h for Teensy 4, kinetis_flexcan.h for Teensy 3.x) plus the .tpp implementation files.

3. Core Concepts
3.1 Two template classes
The library exposes two class templates, one per protocol:

// CAN 2.0
FlexCAN_T4<CAN3, RX_SIZE_256, TX_SIZE_16> myCan;

// CAN FD
FlexCAN_T4FD<CAN3, RX_SIZE_256, TX_SIZE_16> myFD;
Template parameters, in order:

Parameter	Type	Meaning
Bus	CAN_DEV_TABLE	Which physical controller: CAN0/CAN1/CAN2/CAN3 depending on chip (see table above)
RX queue size	FLEXCAN_RXQUEUE_TABLE	Depth of the software receive queue (default RX_SIZE_16)
TX queue size	FLEXCAN_TXQUEUE_TABLE	Depth of the software transmit queue (default TX_SIZE_16)
The queue-size enums (RX_SIZE_2 … RX_SIZE_1024, TX_SIZE_2 … TX_SIZE_1024, all powers of two) are compile-time constants that size an internal Circular_Buffer. Bigger queues cost more RAM but let more frames pile up between calls to events() before anything is dropped. You can give the object any variable name you like, and you can instantiate more than one object for more than one bus.

3.2 Message structures
CAN_message_t — used for CAN 2.0 frames:

typedef struct CAN_message_t {
  uint32_t id = 0;          // CAN identifier
  uint16_t timestamp = 0;   // FlexCAN timer value when the frame arrived
  uint8_t  idhit = 0;       // which filter slot matched
  struct {
    bool extended = 0;      // 29-bit identifier if true
    bool remote = 0;        // remote transmission request
    bool overrun = 0;       // message overrun occurred
    bool reserved = 0;
  } flags;
  uint8_t len = 8;          // data length, 0-8
  uint8_t buf[8] = {0};     // payload
  int8_t  mb = 0;           // mailbox the frame came from
  uint8_t bus = 0;          // originating bus (useful with events())
  bool    seq = 0;          // set true to send as a sequential/ordered frame
} CAN_message_t;
CANFD_message_t — used for CAN FD frames. Same idea, but with a 64-byte payload and two extra control bits:

typedef struct CANFD_message_t {
  uint32_t id = 0;
  uint16_t timestamp = 0;
  uint8_t  idhit = 0;
  bool brs = 1;   // Bit Rate Switch: use the faster FD data rate for the payload
  bool esi = 0;   // Error State Indicator
  bool edl = 1;   // Extended Data Length: 1 = FD frame, 0 = classic CAN 2.0 frame
  struct {
    bool extended = 0;
    bool overrun = 0;
    bool reserved = 0;
  } flags;
  uint8_t len = 8;
  uint8_t buf[64] = {0};
  int8_t  mb = 0;
  uint8_t bus = 0;
  bool    seq = 0;
} CANFD_message_t;
Both brs and edl default to 1. If you leave edl = 1, the frame goes out as a true FD frame; set it to 0 to send a classic CAN 2.0-compatible frame (payload truncated to 8 bytes) from the FD object.

3.3 The base class
Both templates derive from a common abstract base, FlexCAN_T4_Base, which is how the library lets unrelated code (its own ISO-TP module, or a third-party library) hold a generic pointer to "whichever bus object the user created" without knowing if it's CAN 2.0 or FD. You generally don't touch this directly — it exists for interoperability.

4. Quick Start
Minimal CAN 2.0 sketch:

#include <FlexCAN_T4.h>

FlexCAN_T4<CAN1, RX_SIZE_256, TX_SIZE_16> Can1;

void canSniff(const CAN_message_t &msg) {
  Serial.print("MB "); Serial.print(msg.mb);
  Serial.print("  ID: 0x"); Serial.print(msg.id, HEX);
  Serial.print("  LEN: "); Serial.println(msg.len);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Can1.begin();                 // MUST be called first
  Can1.setBaudRate(500000);
  Can1.onReceive(canSniff);     // fires for any interrupt-enabled mailbox
  Can1.enableMBInterrupts();
}

void loop() {
  Can1.events();                // pumps queued RX frames into callbacks
}
Minimal CAN FD sketch (Teensy 4, CAN3 only):

#include <FlexCAN_T4.h>

FlexCAN_T4FD<CAN3, RX_SIZE_256, TX_SIZE_16> FD;

void fdSniff(const CANFD_message_t &msg) {
  Serial.print("ID: 0x"); Serial.print(msg.id, HEX);
  Serial.print("  LEN: "); Serial.println(msg.len);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  FD.begin();

  CANFD_timings_t config;
  config.clock      = CLK_24MHz;
  config.baudrate   = 1000000;   // nominal (arbitration) bitrate
  config.baudrateFD = 2000000;   // data-phase bitrate
  config.propdelay  = 190;
  config.bus_length = 1;
  config.sample     = 75;
  FD.setBaudRate(config);

  FD.onReceive(fdSniff);
  FD.enableMBInterrupts();
}

void loop() {
  FD.events();
}
⚠️ begin() is mandatory before calling anything else. Skipping it will hard-fault the Teensy because the peripheral clock and registers aren't configured yet.

5. Initialization & Baud Rate
5.1 CAN 2.0
myCan.begin();
myCan.setBaudRate(1000000);                       // 1 Mbit/s, normal mode
myCan.setBaudRate(500000, LISTEN_ONLY);            // listen-only / silent monitoring
uint32_t rate = myCan.getBaudRate();               // read back the configured rate
FLEXCAN_RXTX (TX, RX, LISTEN_ONLY) is the second argument on setBaudRate(); passing LISTEN_ONLY puts the controller in a passive monitoring mode that never transmits or acknowledges — handy for bus sniffing without disturbing traffic.

5.2 CAN FD
CAN FD has three ways to set timing, from simplest to most manual:

a) Fixed presets — nominal 1 Mbit/s arbitration phase with a fixed FD data-phase rate:

FD.setBaudRate(CAN_1M_2M);   // 1 Mbit nominal / 2 Mbit data
// also available: CAN_1M_4M, CAN_1M_6M, CAN_1M_8M
b) Custom timings struct — the library computes the bit-timing segments for you:

CANFD_timings_t config;
config.clock      = CLK_24MHz;   // oscillator/reference clock feeding the controller
config.baudrate   = 1000000;     // nominal bitrate            (default 1,000,000)
config.baudrateFD = 2000000;     // FD data-phase bitrate       (default 2,000,000)
config.propdelay  = 190;         // propagation delay, ns       (default 190)
config.bus_length = 1;           // bus length, meters          (default 1)
config.sample     = 75;          // desired sample point, %     (default 75)
FD.setBaudRate(config);
FLEXCAN_CLOCK options: CLK_OFF, CLK_8MHz, CLK_16MHz, CLK_20MHz, CLK_24MHz (default), CLK_30MHz, CLK_40MHz, CLK_60MHz, CLK_80MHz.

c) Advanced/manual selection — when the timing solver finds several valid prescaler/segment combinations for your requested rate, setBaudRateAdvanced() lets you pick specific candidate solutions instead of letting the library auto-select the best one:

FD.setBaudRateAdvanced(config, nominal_choice, flexdata_choice, TX);
This is an expert-level escape hatch; most sketches only need setBaudRate(config).

5.3 Changing the reference clock at runtime (Teensy 4 only)
myCan.setClock(CLK_60MHz);   // available on FlexCAN_T4 (CAN 2.0); default is 24 MHz
6. Pin Selection
Each bus has a default pin pair; you can switch to the alternate pair if your board layout needs it:

myCan.setRX(ALT);   // or DEF (the default)
myCan.setTX(ALT);
FLEXCAN_PINS is just { ALT = 0, DEF = 1 }. Call these before/around begin(), per your board's pinout documentation.

7. Sending & Receiving Frames
7.1 Polling reads
CAN_message_t msg;          // or CANFD_message_t for the FD class
if (myCan.read(msg)) {
  // got a frame
}
read() pulls from whichever interrupt-disabled source has data — if FIFO is enabled, each call randomly services either FIFO or a polled mailbox (whichever isn't interrupt-driven). There are also two more explicit variants:

myCan.readMB(msg);     // read only from a (non-interrupt) mailbox
myCan.readFIFO(msg);   // read only from the FIFO (CAN 2.0 only)
Polling never touches mailboxes/FIFO you've configured for interrupts — those are drained exclusively through callbacks via events().

7.2 Writing frames
myCan.write(msg);            // use any available free TX mailbox
myCan.write(MB15, msg);      // force a specific mailbox (must be configured as TX)
FIFO is receive-only; it can never be used to transmit.

7.3 Sequential (ordered) frames
Bus arbitration normally means multiple queued frames can leave in any order (lowest ID wins arbitration first). If you need strict send ordering:

msg.seq = 1;
myCan.write(msg);
Sequential frames are always funneled through the single, lowest-numbered TX mailbox and queued for the next cycle if that mailbox is busy, guaranteeing they leave in the order you wrote them.

7.4 events()
void loop() {
  myCan.events();
}
events() is the pump that moves frames sitting in the internal RX queue out to your registered callbacks (and, for sequential frames, drains the TX queue in order). Call it frequently — from loop(), an IntervalTimer, or a cooperative thread (e.g. TeensyThreads). If you never register any callbacks and only poll with read()/readMB()/readFIFO(), you don't need events().

8. Mailboxes
8.1 Mailbox identifiers
FLEXCAN_MAILBOX enumerates MB0 through MB63 (Teensy 4 has 64 physical mailboxes in CAN 2.0 mode; Teensy 3.x has 16), plus a special value FIFO = 99 used wherever an API accepts "the FIFO" in place of a specific mailbox number (e.g. filter or interrupt calls).

8.2 Configuring a mailbox's direction/type
myCan.setMB(MB9,  TX);            // transmit mailbox
myCan.setMB(MB10, RX, EXT);       // receive mailbox, accepts extended (29-bit) IDs
myCan.setMB(MB11, RX, STD);       // receive mailbox, accepts standard (11-bit) IDs
The third argument is a FLEXCAN_IDE value (STD is the default). Whatever you set here is also what later filter calls implicitly use — filters don't take a separate standard/extended argument for mailboxes; the mailbox's own configured type governs that.

8.3 Limiting mailbox count (CAN 2.0 only)
myCan.setMaxMB(16);   // only use the first 16 mailboxes
8.4 Introspection
myCan.mailboxStatus();
Prints (over Serial) a breakdown of how many mailboxes are configured as standard RX, extended RX, and TX, whether FIFO is enabled, and how many FIFO filters are available — a good first call after setup to sanity-check your configuration.

9. Callbacks & Interrupts
9.1 Enabling interrupts per mailbox
myCan.enableMBInterrupts();          // all mailboxes
myCan.enableMBInterrupt(MB4);        // just mailbox 4
myCan.enableMBInterrupt(FIFO);       // the FIFO
myCan.disableMBInterrupt(MB4);
myCan.disableMBInterrupts();
9.2 Registering handlers
myCan.onReceive(MB0, canSniff);   // fire only for mailbox 0
myCan.onReceive(MB1, canSniff);   // fire only for mailbox 1
myCan.onReceive(FIFO, canSniff);  // fire only for FIFO frames
myCan.onReceive(canSniff);        // global: fire for ANY mailbox/FIFO frame

// CAN 2.0 only — transmit-confirmation callbacks:
myCan.onTransmit(MB9, txDone);
myCan.onTransmit(txDone);
Handler signature (swap CAN_message_t for CANFD_message_t on the FD class):

void canSniff(const CAN_message_t &msg) {
  Serial.print("MB ");       Serial.print(msg.mb);
  Serial.print("  OVERRUN: ");Serial.print(msg.flags.overrun);
  Serial.print("  LEN: ");   Serial.print(msg.len);
  Serial.print(" EXT: ");    Serial.print(msg.flags.extended);
  Serial.print(" TS: ");     Serial.print(msg.timestamp);
  Serial.print(" ID: ");     Serial.print(msg.id, HEX);
  Serial.print(" Buffer: ");
  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < msg.len; i++) {
    Serial.print(msg.buf[i], HEX); Serial.print(" ");
  }
  Serial.println();
}
Remember callbacks only actually fire when events() is being called regularly.

9.3 Object-oriented callbacks (CAN 2.0 only)
As an alternative to free-function callbacks, you can subclass CANListener and attach the object directly:

class MyListener : public CANListener {
  bool frameHandler(CAN_message_t &frame, int mailbox, uint8_t controller) override {
    // handle frame, return true if consumed
    return true;
  }
};

MyListener listener;

void setup() {
  myCan.begin();
  myCan.attachObj(&listener);
  listener.attachGeneralHandler();      // or listener.attachMBHandler(MB0) for one mailbox
}
Use detachObj() / detachMBHandler() / detachGeneralHandler() to unregister. Up to 4 listener objects can be attached per bus (SIZE_LISTENERS = 4). This mechanism exists only on the CAN 2.0 class, not on FlexCAN_T4FD.

9.4 External weak functions
For interop with other libraries that want raw, unfiltered access to every received frame (e.g. the companion TeensyCAN library), the header declares three weak extern hooks per protocol:

extern void ext_output1(const CAN_message_t &msg);
extern void ext_output2(const CAN_message_t &msg);
extern void ext_output3(const CAN_message_t &msg);   // used by TeensyCAN

extern void ext_outputFD1(const CANFD_message_t &msg);
extern void ext_outputFD2(const CANFD_message_t &msg);
extern void ext_outputFD3(const CANFD_message_t &msg); // used by TeensyCAN
Define any of these in your own sketch/library to tap into the interrupt-time data stream, independent of the mailbox/FIFO filtering system.

10. The Filtering System
FlexCAN's native ID filtering is powerful but fiddly at the register level (raw ID + mask pairs). FlexCAN_T4 wraps it in a much friendlier API and automatically calculates the hardware masks for you.

10.1 Start from a clean slate
Best practice is to block everything first, then explicitly allow what you want:

myCan.setMBFilter(REJECT_ALL);     // for mailboxes
myCan.setFIFOFilter(REJECT_ALL);   // for FIFO
(FLEXCAN_FLTEN is { ACCEPT_ALL = 0, REJECT_ALL = 1 }.)

10.2 Mailbox filters
myCan.setMBFilter(MB6, 0x123);                       // accept only ID 0x123
myCan.setMBFilter(MB6, 0x123, 0x456);                // up to 5 IDs per mailbox
myCan.setMBFilterRange(MB7, 0x1, 0x9);               // accept a contiguous ID range
myCan.setMBFilter(MB6, ACCEPT_ALL);                  // or REJECT_ALL — per-mailbox on/off
For more control, the CAN 2.0 class also exposes:

myCan.setMBUserFilter(MB6, 0x123, 0x7FF);            // explicit ID + your own mask
myCan.setMBManualFilter(MB6, 0x123, 0x7FF);          // raw, lowest-level filter write
(These two are CAN 2.0-only; the FD class relies on setMBFilter/setMBFilterRange.)

10.3 FIFO filters (CAN 2.0 only)
myCan.setFIFOFilter(0, 0x123, STD);                  // filter 0: standard ID 0x123
myCan.setFIFOFilter(1, 0x456, EXT);                  // filter 1: extended ID 0x456
myCan.setFIFOFilter(2, 0x100, 0x200, STD);           // one filter, two IDs
myCan.setFIFOFilterRange(3, 0x300, 0x310, STD);      // one filter, ID range
FLEXCAN_IDE values used here: STD, EXT, RTR, NONE. An optional trailing remote argument (default NONE) lets a filter specifically match/exclude Remote Transmission Request frames.

Related configuration:

myCan.setRFFN(RFFN_16);            // how many FIFO ID filters are available (8..128, steps of 8)
myCan.setFIFOFilterTable(B);       // choose the underlying hardware filter table format (A/B/C)
myCan.setFIFOUserFilter(...);      // explicit mask variants, mirroring setMBUserFilter
myCan.setFIFOManualFilter(...);    // raw, lowest-level variant
Run mailboxStatus() after enabling FIFO to see exactly how many filter slots you have to work with, since it depends on setRFFN().

10.4 Enhanced filtering (bleed-through suppression)
Because the CAN 2.0 filtering hardware works with ID+mask pairs, requesting a small set of specific IDs (say, 0x1 and 0x5) can also let other IDs through if they happen to share the same masked bit pattern (here, 0x3 and 0x7 would also match, since only 2 of the ID's bits actually differ between your two requested IDs). To have the library discard those unwanted bleed-through frames after the hardware pass:

myCan.enhanceFilter(MB6);   // per-mailbox
myCan.enhanceFilter(FIFO);  // for FIFO
10.5 Distribution
By default, when several mailboxes/filters could all match the same incoming ID, the FIFO/mailbox that grabs it first "wins" and the others never see it — a problem if you have multiple independent callbacks expecting the same frame. distribute() fixes this by copying the frame into every queue whose filter matches:

myCan.distribute();          // enable
myCan.distribute(false);     // disable
11. FIFO Mode (CAN 2.0 only)
The RX FIFO is a hardware first-in-first-out receive queue that occupies the first several mailboxes' worth of RAM (freeing you from managing individual mailboxes for bulk reception). It exists on both Teensy 3.x and the CAN 2.0 controllers on Teensy 4, but not on the CAN FD controller.

myCan.enableFIFO();             // turn FIFO on
myCan.disableFIFO();
myCan.enableFIFOInterrupt();    // drive FIFO reception via interrupt + callback
myCan.disableFIFOInterrupt();
myCan.setMRP(true);             // mailbox(1) vs FIFO(0) arbitration priority
myCan.setRRS(true);             // also store remote-request frames
FIFO is receive-only (see §7.2) and is read either through readFIFO() (polling) or an onReceive(FIFO, handler) callback (interrupt-driven).

12. CAN FD-Specific Configuration
12.1 Mailbox regions and payload size
Unlike CAN 2.0's fixed 8-byte mailboxes, FD mailbox RAM is carved into "regions" whose per-mailbox payload size you choose — bigger payloads per mailbox means fewer total mailboxes, since they share the same RAM:

myFD.setRegions(8);    // default: 64 mailboxes, 8-byte payload each
myFD.setRegions(64);   // 14 mailboxes, 64-byte (full FD) payload each
Valid sizes are 8, 16, 32, or 64 bytes. The call returns the actual mailbox count you end up with, so you can check it at runtime. There's also a two-argument form to size the two hardware regions independently:

myFD.setRegions(mbdsr0, mbdsr1);   // configure the two payload regions separately
12.2 Frame-level FD flags
Recall from §3.2: CANFD_message_t carries brs (bit-rate switching — use the fast data-phase rate) and edl (extended data length — send as a true FD frame vs. a truncated CAN 2.0-compatible frame), both defaulted to 1.

12.3 What's missing relative to CAN 2.0
FIFO, setMaxMB(), onTransmit(), CANListener/attachObj(), error(), getRXQueueCount()/getTXQueueCount(), enableLoopBack(), setMRP()/setRRS(), setMBUserFilter()/setMBManualFilter(), and a public reset() are all CAN 2.0-only in this library — see the matrix below.

13. Error Handling & Diagnostics
CAN 2.0 objects can report bus error/state information:

CAN_error_t err;
if (myCan.error(err, true)) {   // 'true' also prints a human-readable report to Serial
  // inspect err.* fields yourself if needed
}
CAN_error_t fields:

Field	Type	Meaning
state	char[30]	Text description of controller state (defaults to "Idle")
BIT1_ERR, BIT0_ERR	bool	Bit-level transmission errors
ACK_ERR	bool	No node acknowledged the frame
CRC_ERR	bool	CRC mismatch
FRM_ERR	bool	Form/framing error
STF_ERR	bool	Bit-stuffing error
TX_WRN, RX_WRN	bool	TX/RX error-warning thresholds reached
FLT_CONF	char[14]	Fault confinement state text
RX_ERR_COUNTER, TX_ERR_COUNTER	uint8_t	Live error counters
ESR1	uint32_t	Raw Error & Status Register 1
ECR	uint16_t	Raw Error Counter Register
This diagnostic API is CAN 2.0-only in the current header.

Queue depth introspection (also CAN 2.0-only):

uint32_t pending_rx = myCan.getRXQueueCount();
uint32_t pending_tx = myCan.getTXQueueCount();
14. Loopback, DMA, and Reset
myCan.enableLoopBack();     // CAN 2.0 only: internal loopback for self-test without a bus
myCan.enableDMA();          // both classes: DMA-assisted mailbox transfers
myCan.disableDMA();
myCan.reset();              // CAN 2.0 only (public): soft-reset the controller
On the FD class, reset() exists internally but is private — it isn't part of the public API.

15. CAN 2.0 vs CAN FD — Feature Matrix
Feature	FlexCAN_T4 (CAN 2.0)	FlexCAN_T4FD (CAN FD)
Hardware RX FIFO	✅	❌ (not available on Teensy 4's FD controller)
onTransmit() callback	✅	❌
CANListener / attachObj()	✅	❌
error() / CAN_error_t	✅	❌
getRXQueueCount() / getTXQueueCount()	✅	❌
enableLoopBack()	✅	❌
setMaxMB()	✅	❌ (use setRegions() instead)
setMRP() / setRRS()	✅	❌
Public reset()	✅	❌ (private)
setMBUserFilter() / setMBManualFilter()	✅	❌
getBaudRate()	✅	❌
setRegions() (payload-vs-mailbox-count tradeoff)	—	✅
brs / esi / edl frame flags	—	✅
setBaudRateAdvanced()	—	✅
setMB(), setMBFilter()/setMBFilterRange(), enhanceFilter(), distribute(), enableDMA(), events(), onReceive(), mailboxStatus(), write()/read()/readMB()	✅	✅
(Reflects the header as reviewed; the library is actively developed, so check the latest FlexCAN_T4.h if a method listed here seems to be missing or a new one has appeared.)

